      experienceLevel: "FRESHER",
      difficulty: "EASY",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Machine learning is a field of computing in which algorithms learn patterns from data to make predictions, classifications, rankings, or decisions...",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Machine learning is a field of computing in which algorithms learn patterns from data to make predictions, classifications, rankings, or decisions without every rule being explicitly programmed. A model is trained using data and an objective, then evaluated on data it did not use for fitting. Good ML work includes data quality, feature/representation design, evaluation, and monitoring-not just model selection.",
      detailedAnswer: "Machine learning is a way of building systems that learn patterns from data and use those learned patterns to make predictions, classifications, recommendations, or decisions instead of relying entirely on manually written rules.\n\nIn traditional rule-based programming, the developer explicitly defines the logic.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to detect whether a transaction is suspicious:\n\nif amount > 100000 AND country != user's_country:\n    flag_as_suspicious()\n\nThe developer decides the rules, thresholds, and conditions.\n\nIn machine learning, we provide historical examples such as:\n\ntransaction features -> legitimate/fraud label\n\nThe model learns statistical patterns from those examples. For a new transaction, it predicts the probability that the transaction is fraudulent.\n\nThe key difference is where the decision logic comes from:\n\nTraditional programming:\nData + Rules -> Output\n\nMachine learning:\nData + Expected outputs -> Learned model\n\nThen:\nNew data + Learned model -> Prediction\n\nMachine learning is particularly useful when the rules are difficult to explicitly describe. Examples include:\n- Image recognition.\n- Spam detection.\n- Fraud detection.\n- Recommendation systems.\n- Speech recognition.\n- Demand forecasting.\n- Predictive maintenance.\n\nHowever, machine learning is not automatically better than rules. If a requirement is deterministic and easy to express, explicit rules may be simpler, more explainable, and easier to maintain.\n\nFor example, calculating whether an employee is eligible for a benefit based on a clearly defined policy may be better implemented using normal business rules rather than a machine-learning model.\n\nA production system can also combine both approaches. For example, an ML model can produce a fraud score, while deterministic rules enforce hard business constraints such as blocking a transaction from a disabled account.\n\nThe interview-ready answer is: Machine learning learns patterns from historical data to make predictions, whereas rule-based programming requires developers to explicitly encode the decision logic. ML is valuable when patterns are complex or difficult to define manually, while deterministic rules are often better for simple, explicit business logic.",
