      shortDescription: "In rule-based programming, humans explicitly define rules that map inputs to outputs",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "In rule-based programming, humans explicitly define rules that map inputs to outputs. In machine learning, the system learns a mapping or decision boundary from examples by optimizing an objective. ML is useful when the rules are difficult to specify manually, but it introduces dependence on data quality, statistical assumptions, and monitoring.",
      detailedAnswer: "The main difference is how the system's decision logic is created and adapted.\n\nIn traditional rule-based programming, developers explicitly define the rules that transform input into output.\n\nFor example:\n\nif temperature > 38:\n    diagnosis = \"fever\"\nelse:\n    diagnosis = \"normal\"\n\nThe behavior is determined by rules written by developers.\n\nIn machine learning, the developer defines a model, training process, features, objective, and data pipeline, while the model learns the relationship between inputs and outputs from data.\n\nConceptually:\n\nRule-based:\nInput + Handwritten rules -> Output\n\nMachine learning:\nTraining data + Learning algorithm -> Model\n\nThen:\nInput + Model -> Prediction\n\nKey differences include:\n\n1. Logic creation\n\nRule-based systems use manually defined rules. ML systems learn parameters/patterns from data.\n\n2. Adaptability\n\nA rule-based system normally requires a developer to change rules when behavior changes. An ML system can potentially be retrained using new data.\n\n3. Explainability\n\nSimple rules are usually easier to explain. Some ML models, especially complex neural networks, can be harder to interpret.\n\n4. Data dependency\n\nTraditional rules can work with little historical data. ML generally needs representative training data, although the amount depends heavily on the problem and model.\n\n5. Handling complexity\n\nRules are excellent when business logic is explicit and deterministic. ML can be useful when the relationship between inputs and outcomes is complex and difficult to encode manually.\n\n6. Testing\n\nRule-based systems can often be tested against exact expected outputs. ML systems require evaluation against datasets and statistical metrics because predictions may be probabilistic.\n\n7. Maintenance\n\nRule systems are maintained by changing rules. ML systems require monitoring for data quality, distribution changes, model degradation, and retraining.\n\nFor example, an email spam detector could be implemented using hundreds of manually written rules, but maintaining those rules becomes difficult as spam behavior changes. An ML model can learn patterns from historical spam and legitimate email and be retrained as new patterns appear.\n\nIn production, the two approaches are often combined. Hard safety or business constraints can remain deterministic while ML handles prediction or ranking.\n\nThe interview-ready answer is: Rule-based programming explicitly encodes decision logic, while machine learning learns that logic from data. Rule-based systems are often preferable for deterministic policies, while ML is useful for complex patterns that are difficult to specify manually.",
