      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "AI is the broad field of building systems that perform tasks associated with intelligent behavior",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "AI is the broad field of building systems that perform tasks associated with intelligent behavior. Machine learning is a subset of AI that learns patterns from data. Deep learning is a subset of ML based primarily on multi-layer neural networks that can learn useful representations from large datasets.",
      detailedAnswer: "AI, machine learning, and deep learning are related concepts at different levels of scope.\n\nArtificial Intelligence (AI) is the broadest concept. It refers to systems designed to perform tasks that normally require aspects of human intelligence, such as reasoning, planning, perception, language understanding, decision-making, or problem solving.\n\nMachine Learning (ML) is a subset of AI. Instead of explicitly programming every decision rule, ML systems learn patterns or parameters from data.\n\nDeep Learning (DL) is a subset of machine learning that primarily uses multi-layer neural networks to learn representations and complex relationships from data.\n\nThe relationship can be represented as:\n\nAI\n+-- Machine Learning\n    +-- Deep Learning\n\nExamples:\n\nAI without necessarily using ML:\n- A rule-based expert system.\n- A search/planning system using explicitly designed algorithms.\n\nMachine learning:\n- Linear regression.\n- Logistic regression.\n- Decision trees.\n- Random forests.\n- Gradient boosting.\n- Support vector machines.\n- Neural networks.\n\nDeep learning:\n- Deep feed-forward neural networks.\n- Convolutional neural networks.\n- Transformers.\n- Recurrent neural networks and related architectures.\n\nOne important distinction is that not all AI is machine learning, and not all machine learning is deep learning.\n\nFor example, a chess system using manually designed search and evaluation rules can be considered AI without necessarily being an ML system.\n\nA traditional ML fraud model might use engineered transaction features with gradient boosting. A deep-learning system might learn useful representations directly from large amounts of complex data such as images, audio, or text.\n\nDeep learning has become especially important for areas such as:\n- Computer vision.\n- Speech processing.\n- Natural language processing.\n- Generative AI.\n- Multimodal systems.\n\nThe trade-off is that deep-learning models can require substantial computational resources, large or carefully curated datasets, sophisticated training pipelines, and strong monitoring.\n\nThe interview-ready answer is: AI is the broad field of intelligent systems, ML is a subset of AI that learns from data, and deep learning is a subset of ML based primarily on multi-layer neural networks.",
