      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Supervised learning uses labeled examples with a target to learn predictions, such as classification or regression",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Supervised learning uses labeled examples with a target to learn predictions, such as classification or regression. Unsupervised learning works without a target label and seeks structure such as clusters, latent representations, or anomalies. The choice depends on whether reliable target labels exist and what business problem must be solved.",
      detailedAnswer: "The main difference is whether the training data contains known target labels.\n\nSupervised learning uses labeled examples. Each training example contains input features and a known target value.\n\nFor example:\n\nInput:\n- transaction amount\n- location\n- device type\n- transaction frequency\n\nTarget:\n- fraud = yes/no\n\nThe model learns a mapping from inputs to the target.\n\nCommon supervised tasks include:\n- Classification: predict a category, such as spam/not spam.\n- Regression: predict a numeric value, such as house price.\n\nExamples of supervised algorithms include:\n- Linear regression.\n- Logistic regression.\n- Decision trees.\n- Random forests.\n- Gradient boosting.\n- Neural networks.\n\nUnsupervised learning works with data where there is no explicit target label. The algorithm attempts to discover structure or patterns in the data.\n\nFor example, a company may have customer information but no predefined customer segments. Clustering can discover groups of customers with similar behavior.\n\nCommon unsupervised tasks include:\n- Clustering.\n- Dimensionality reduction.\n- Anomaly detection in some formulations.\n- Representation learning.\n\nExamples include:\n- K-means.\n- Hierarchical clustering.\n- DBSCAN.\n- PCA.\n- Autoencoders for certain representation/anomaly-detection applications.\n\nExample:\n\nSupervised:\nCustomer features -> known segment label -> train model.\n\nUnsupervised:\nCustomer features -> discover groups.\n\nA practical challenge with unsupervised learning is evaluation. Since there may be no ground-truth labels, the usefulness of discovered structure often requires domain knowledge or downstream business validation.\n\nThere are also approaches between and beyond these categories, such as semi-supervised learning and self-supervised learning.\n\nThe interview-ready answer is: Supervised learning learns from labeled input-output examples for tasks such as classification and regression, while unsupervised learning works without explicit target labels to discover patterns or structure in data.",
