      shortDescription: "Semi-supervised learning combines a relatively small labeled dataset with a larger unlabeled dataset",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Semi-supervised learning combines a relatively small labeled dataset with a larger unlabeled dataset. It is useful when obtaining labels is expensive but collecting raw data is easy. The method must be designed carefully because incorrect assumptions about unlabeled examples can reinforce errors.",
      detailedAnswer: "Semi-supervised learning is a machine-learning approach that uses a relatively small amount of labeled data together with a larger amount of unlabeled data during training.\n\nThe motivation is practical: obtaining labels can be expensive, while collecting raw data may be relatively easy.\n\nFor example, imagine a company has:\n\n10,000 labeled images\n500,000 unlabeled images\n\nA semi-supervised approach can use both sets rather than discarding the unlabeled images.\n\nConceptually:\n\nSmall labeled dataset\n        +\nLarge unlabeled dataset\n        |\n        v\nSemi-supervised training\n        |\n        v\nImproved model\n\nCommon approaches include:\n\n1. Pseudo-labeling\n\nThe model predicts labels for high-confidence unlabeled examples. Those predictions can then be used as additional training targets, with appropriate safeguards.\n\n2. Consistency regularization\n\nThe model is encouraged to produce consistent predictions when the same unlabeled example is subjected to suitable perturbations.\n\n3. Graph-based methods\n\nExamples can be represented as nodes in a graph, with relationships between similar examples helping propagate information from labeled to unlabeled data.\n\nA typical example is medical image classification, where expert annotation is expensive. A small number of images may be carefully labeled by experts while thousands or millions of other images remain unlabeled.\n\nSemi-supervised learning is different from supervised learning because not all training examples have labels. It is different from unsupervised learning because labeled examples are explicitly used to guide learning.\n\nPotential risks include:\n- Incorrect pseudo-labels reinforcing model errors.\n- Poor assumptions about the unlabeled data distribution.\n- Distribution mismatch between labeled and unlabeled data.\n- Confirmation bias in self-training approaches.\n\nTherefore, validation should remain based on a trusted labeled dataset that is separate from the training process.\n\nThe interview-ready answer is: Semi-supervised learning combines a smaller labeled dataset with a larger unlabeled dataset to improve learning when obtaining labels is expensive.",
