      shortDescription: "Reinforcement learning learns behavior through interaction with an environment",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Reinforcement learning learns behavior through interaction with an environment. An agent takes actions, receives rewards or penalties, and learns a policy intended to maximize cumulative reward. Unlike ordinary supervised learning, there is not necessarily a correct labeled action for every state.",
      detailedAnswer: "Reinforcement learning (RL) is a machine-learning paradigm in which an agent learns how to make decisions by interacting with an environment and receiving rewards or penalties based on its actions.\n\nThe basic interaction is:\n\nAgent -> Action -> Environment\nAgent <- Reward + New state <- Environment\n\nThe agent's objective is generally to learn a policy that maximizes expected cumulative reward over time rather than simply predicting a labeled target for each input.\n\nKey concepts include:\n\n1. State\n\nThe current situation observed by the agent.\n\n2. Action\n\nA decision the agent can take.\n\n3. Reward\n\nFeedback received after taking an action.\n\n4. Policy\n\nA strategy mapping states to actions or action probabilities.\n\n5. Environment\n\nThe system with which the agent interacts.\n\n6. Value function\n\nAn estimate of how valuable a state or action is in terms of expected future reward.\n\nFor example, in a game:\n\nState -> current board position\nAction -> move a piece\nReward -> positive reward for winning, negative reward for losing\n\nThe agent learns through repeated interaction.\n\nA key challenge is the exploration-versus-exploitation trade-off.\n\nExploration means trying actions to discover potentially better strategies.\n\nExploitation means choosing actions that the agent currently believes are good.\n\nToo much exploration wastes opportunities, while too little exploration can prevent the agent from discovering better behavior.\n\nCommon RL algorithms include Q-learning, SARSA, policy-gradient methods, actor-critic methods, and deep reinforcement learning approaches.\n\nReinforcement learning differs from supervised learning because there is usually no direct label saying 'this action is correct.' Instead, the agent receives feedback through rewards, often with delayed consequences.\n\nFor example, an agent might make several decisions before receiving a final reward. The learning algorithm must determine which earlier decisions contributed to that outcome.\n\nRL has been applied to:\n- Games.\n- Robotics.\n- Resource allocation.\n- Recommendation/ranking research.\n- Control systems.\n- Scheduling and optimization problems.\n\nIn production, RL can be significantly more difficult than offline supervised learning because exploration can have real-world cost or risk. Simulation, offline evaluation, safety constraints, and carefully designed reward functions are often important.\n\nThe interview-ready answer is: Reinforcement learning trains an agent through interaction with an environment, using rewards and penalties to learn a policy that maximizes long-term cumulative reward.",
