      subcategorySlug: "generalization-and-model-quality",
      experienceLevel: "FRESHER",
      difficulty: "EASY",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Overfitting occurs when a model learns training-specific patterns, including noise, so its training performance is strong but its performance on unseen...",
      explanation: "This reveals whether the candidate understands why a model generalizes poorly and how to improve it rather than just naming an algorithm.",
      sampleAnswer: "Overfitting occurs when a model learns training-specific patterns, including noise, so its training performance is strong but its performance on unseen data is substantially worse. It can result from excessive model complexity, too little data, leakage, or overly aggressive tuning against the validation set.",
      detailedAnswer: "Overfitting occurs when a machine-learning model learns the training data too closely, including noise or accidental patterns, and therefore performs poorly on unseen data.\n\nFor example, suppose a model achieves:\n\nTraining accuracy: 99%\nValidation accuracy: 72%\n\nThis large gap can indicate overfitting.\n\nA model that generalizes well should learn patterns that apply beyond the training examples rather than memorizing the training dataset.\n\nConceptually:\n\nTraining data -> model learns useful patterns + noise\n\nIf the model relies heavily on noise or training-specific details, performance decreases on new examples.\n\nCommon causes include:\n- Model is too complex for the amount of training data.\n- Training dataset is too small.\n- Training data contains noise.\n- Excessive training for some model types.\n- Data leakage or poor dataset construction.\n- Weak regularization.\n- Highly flexible models with insufficient constraints.\n\nFor example, a decision tree can keep splitting until it nearly memorizes the training examples. It may achieve excellent training accuracy but perform poorly on unseen data.\n\nOverfitting is not simply 'high model complexity.' A complex model can generalize well when there is sufficient data and appropriate regularization. The actual concern is poor generalization.\n\nI would evaluate overfitting using a proper train/validation/test setup or cross-validation. The test set should be kept separate until final evaluation.\n\nA useful mental model is:\n\nUnderfitting -> model has not learned enough.\n\nGood fit -> model captures useful general patterns.\n\nOverfitting -> model learns training-specific details and noise.\n\nThe interview-ready answer is: Overfitting happens when a model performs very well on training data but poorly on unseen data because it has learned training-specific patterns or noise instead of generalizable relationships.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate understands the bias-variance trade-off and can explain how regularization controls overfitting.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
      followUpQuestions: ["How do you detect overfitting?", "How can overfitting be reduced?", "When would you prefer high recall over high precision?"],
      tags: ["machine-learning", "overfitting"],
      seoTitle: "What is overfitting? | Machine Learning Interview Question",
      seoDescription: "Overfitting occurs when a model learns training-specific patterns, including noise, so its training performance is strong but its performance on unseen...",
