      explanation: "This reveals whether the candidate understands why a model generalizes poorly and how to improve it rather than just naming an algorithm.",
      sampleAnswer: "Use more representative data when possible, reduce unnecessary model complexity, apply regularization, early stopping, feature selection, data augmentation where appropriate, and cross-validation. Also check for leakage and distribution differences before changing the model. The right remedy depends on why the model is overfitting.",
      detailedAnswer: "Overfitting can be reduced by improving the amount and quality of training data, controlling model complexity, applying regularization, and validating the model correctly.\n\nCommon techniques include:\n\n1. Collect more representative data\n\nMore diverse training examples can help the model learn general patterns instead of memorizing a small dataset. More data is not automatically useful if it is biased, duplicated, or unrepresentative.\n\n2. Data augmentation\n\nFor suitable domains such as images or audio, controlled transformations can create additional training examples while preserving the target.\n\n3. Regularization\n\nRegularization discourages overly complex solutions. Examples include L1 and L2 regularization.\n\nL1 can encourage sparse parameter values, while L2 penalizes large weights.\n\n4. Reduce model complexity\n\nExamples include:\n- Shallower decision trees.\n- Fewer parameters/features where appropriate.\n- Smaller neural networks.\n- Limiting tree depth.\n\n5. Early stopping\n\nFor iterative models such as neural networks and gradient boosting, stop training when validation performance stops improving.\n\n6. Dropout\n\nIn neural networks, dropout randomly disables selected units during training, which can act as a regularization technique.\n\n7. Cross-validation\n\nCross-validation provides a more reliable estimate of generalization and helps with model/hyperparameter selection.\n\n8. Feature selection\n\nRemove irrelevant, noisy, or redundant features when they contribute to overfitting.\n\n9. Tune hyperparameters\n\nUse validation or cross-validation to select parameters such as tree depth, regularization strength, learning rate, or model size.\n\n10. Prevent data leakage\n\nA model that accidentally sees target information or future information can appear extremely accurate without actually generalizing. Data preprocessing and feature engineering should be performed using training data appropriately within the validation pipeline.\n\n11. Simplify the problem when appropriate\n\nSometimes a simpler model provides similar validation performance with better interpretability and operational stability.\n\nThe important point is that I would not blindly apply every technique. I would first identify whether the problem is actually overfitting and then choose the intervention based on the model and data.\n\nFor example, if a neural network has very low training loss but increasing validation loss, early stopping, stronger regularization, data augmentation, or a smaller model may help.\n\nThe interview-ready answer is: Reduce overfitting through more representative data, appropriate regularization, simpler models, feature selection, early stopping, data augmentation where appropriate, and disciplined validation that prevents leakage.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate understands the bias-variance trade-off and can explain how regularization controls overfitting.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
      followUpQuestions: ["When can PCA hurt model performance?", "What is overfitting?", "How do you detect overfitting?"],
      tags: ["machine-learning", "overfitting", "reduced"],
      seoTitle: "How can overfitting be reduced? | Machine Learning Interview Question",
      seoDescription: "Use more representative data when possible, reduce unnecessary model complexity, apply regularization, early stopping, feature selection, data...",
      isPublished: true,
