      subcategorySlug: "evaluation-and-validation",
      experienceLevel: "FRESHER",
      difficulty: "EASY",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Cross-validation repeatedly splits training data into folds, trains on some folds, and evaluates on the held-out fold",
      explanation: "This checks practical model assessment skills and whether the candidate can avoid relying on a misleading metric.",
      sampleAnswer: "Cross-validation repeatedly splits training data into folds, trains on some folds, and evaluates on the held-out fold. K-fold cross-validation averages performance across folds and provides a more stable estimate than a single split when data is limited. Preprocessing and feature selection must be performed inside each training fold to avoid leakage.",
      detailedAnswer: "Cross-validation is a model-evaluation and model-selection technique that repeatedly splits the available training data into training and validation portions so the model can be evaluated across multiple data splits.\n\nThe most common form is k-fold cross-validation.\n\nFor 5-fold cross-validation:\n\n1. Split the training dataset into 5 folds.\n2. Train on folds 1-4 and validate on fold 5.\n3. Train on folds 1,2,3,5 and validate on fold 4.\n4. Continue until every fold has been used as validation once.\n5. Average the validation metrics.\n\nConceptually:\n\nFold 1 -> train on 2,3,4,5 -> validate on 1\nFold 2 -> train on 1,3,4,5 -> validate on 2\n...\nFold 5 -> train on 1,2,3,4 -> validate on 5\n\nThis provides a more stable estimate of generalization than relying on a single arbitrary train/validation split, especially when the dataset is relatively small.\n\nCross-validation is commonly used for:\n- Comparing models.\n- Hyperparameter tuning.\n- Estimating model performance.\n- Detecting unstable models.\n\nFor classification, stratified k-fold is often preferred when class proportions need to be preserved across folds.\n\nFor time-series data, ordinary random k-fold cross-validation can be inappropriate because it may train on future observations and validate on past observations. Time-aware splits should be used instead.\n\nFor grouped data, such as multiple records belonging to the same customer, random row-level splitting can cause leakage. Group-aware cross-validation can keep related records in the same fold.\n\nOne important rule is that the final test set should remain untouched during model selection. A common workflow is:\n\nTraining data -> cross-validation for model/hyperparameter selection\n\nFinal held-out test set -> one-time final evaluation\n\nCross-validation can be computationally expensive because the model is trained multiple times.\n\nThe interview-ready answer is: Cross-validation evaluates a model across multiple training/validation splits to obtain a more reliable estimate of generalization and to support model or hyperparameter selection, while the final test set should remain separate.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate can choose an evaluation method that matches the prediction task and explain what the metric says about model behavior.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
