      shortDescription: "Any preprocessing step that learns parameters from data-such as scaling, imputation, feature selection, or dimensionality reduction-must learn those...",
      explanation: "This checks practical model assessment skills and whether the candidate can avoid relying on a misleading metric.",
      sampleAnswer: "Any preprocessing step that learns parameters from data-such as scaling, imputation, feature selection, or dimensionality reduction-must learn those parameters from training data only. Using validation or test data to fit preprocessing leaks information about the evaluation set and makes performance estimates overly optimistic.",
      detailedAnswer: "Preprocessing should be fitted only on training data to prevent information from the validation or test set from influencing the learned transformation. Otherwise, information from data that is supposed to represent unseen examples can leak into the training process, producing overly optimistic evaluation results.\n\nConsider standardization:\n\nx_scaled = (x - mean) / standard_deviation\n\nThe mean and standard deviation must be calculated from the training data only.\n\nCorrect workflow:\n\nTraining data -> fit scaler -> transform training data\n\nValidation data -> use the same fitted scaler -> transform validation data\n\nTest data -> use the same fitted scaler -> transform test data\n\nIncorrect workflow:\n\nTraining + validation + test -> calculate global mean/std -> train model\n\nThe second approach allows information about the evaluation datasets to influence the transformation.\n\nThe same principle applies to:\n- Standardization.\n- Min-max scaling.\n- Imputation values.\n- Feature selection.\n- PCA.\n- Target/frequency encoding.\n- Vocabulary construction.\n- Many other learned preprocessing steps.\n\nFor example, suppose missing values are filled with the median. The median should be learned from the training set. Using the median calculated from the entire dataset allows information from validation/test examples to influence training.\n\nWith cross-validation, preprocessing must be fitted separately inside each training fold and then applied to that fold's validation portion.\n\nA pipeline is useful because it makes this boundary explicit. Conceptually:\n\nPipeline:\nPreprocessor.fit(training_fold)\nPreprocessor.transform(training_fold)\nPreprocessor.transform(validation_fold)\nModel.fit(transformed_training_fold)\nModel.predict(transformed_validation_fold)\n\nThis prevents accidental leakage during hyperparameter tuning and cross-validation.\n\nA subtle point is that deterministic transformations that do not learn anything from the dataset, such as converting a known unit from Celsius to Fahrenheit, do not have the same fitting concern. The rule applies to transformations whose parameters are learned from data.\n\nThe interview-ready answer is: Fit learned preprocessing only on training data because validation and test data must remain unseen during model development. The fitted transformation is then reused unchanged on validation and test data.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate can choose an evaluation method that matches the prediction task and explain what the metric says about model behavior.\\n\\n"],
