      subcategorySlug: "preprocessing-and-feature-engineering",
      experienceLevel: "FRESHER",
      difficulty: "MEDIUM",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "First determine why values are missing and whether missingness itself carries information",
      explanation: "Preprocessing choices often determine whether a model is reliable in production, so interviewers use these questions to test practical judgment.",
      sampleAnswer: "First determine why values are missing and whether missingness itself carries information. Options include dropping rows/columns when justified, imputing with statistics or model-based methods, adding missing indicators, or using models that support missing values. Fit learned imputers on training data only and ensure the strategy is consistent with production data generation.",
      detailedAnswer: "Missing values should be handled based on why the data is missing, how much is missing, the feature type, and the model being used. I would first measure the missing-value percentage per feature and check whether missingness has a meaningful pattern.\n\nFor numerical features, common approaches are mean or median imputation. Median is usually safer when the feature is skewed or contains outliers. For categorical features, I can use the most frequent category or introduce an explicit value such as 'Unknown' or 'Missing'. For time-series data, forward-fill or backward-fill may be appropriate when the business meaning supports it.\n\nI would also consider dropping a feature or rows when the amount of missing data is very high and the feature does not provide enough value. However, I would avoid blindly deleting data because the missingness itself can contain information.\n\nFor some algorithms, missing values can be handled natively, while others require preprocessing. In a production ML pipeline, the imputation strategy must be fitted only on the training data and then applied unchanged to validation, test, and production data. This prevents data leakage.\n\nExample:\n```python\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\nnumeric_pipeline = Pipeline([\n    ('imputer', SimpleImputer(strategy='median')),\n    ('scaler', StandardScaler())\n])\n```\n\nA common mistake is calculating the mean or median using the entire dataset before splitting it into train and test sets. That allows information from the test set to influence training.\n\nIn an interview, I would summarize it as: first understand the missingness, then choose an appropriate strategy rather than automatically filling everything with zero.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate understands how preprocessing affects model quality, data leakage, and the consistency of training and inference.\\n\\n"],
