      sampleAnswer: "Standardization is particularly useful for models sensitive to feature scale, including many linear models, logistic regression, SVMs, neural networks, and distance-based methods such as K-means. Tree-based models generally do not require scaling because splits depend on ordering rather than Euclidean distance or coefficient magnitude.",
      detailedAnswer: "Features should generally be standardized when their numerical scales differ significantly and the algorithm is sensitive to feature magnitude. Standardization usually transforms a feature so that it has approximately zero mean and unit variance.\n\nIt is particularly important for algorithms based on distances, gradients, or regularization. Examples include logistic regression, linear regression with regularization, SVMs, k-nearest neighbors, k-means clustering, PCA, and many neural networks.\n\nFor example, suppose one feature is age ranging from 18 to 80 while another is annual income ranging from 20,000 to 2,000,000. Without scaling, income can dominate distance calculations or affect gradient-based optimization simply because its numerical magnitude is much larger.\n\nTree-based models such as decision trees, random forests, and many gradient-boosted tree implementations generally do not require standardization because their splits depend on feature ordering rather than Euclidean distance or gradient magnitude in the same way.\n\nStandardization should be fitted only on the training data. The same mean and standard deviation are then used to transform validation, test, and production data.\n\nExample:\n```python\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\nscaler = StandardScaler()\nX_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)\nX_test_scaled = scaler.transform(X_test)\n```\n\nA common mistake is fitting the scaler separately on the test set. That creates inconsistent preprocessing and can leak information from the test distribution.\n\nThe interview-ready rule is: scale when the model is sensitive to feature magnitude; don't add scaling automatically when the algorithm does not need it.",
