      shortDescription: "Terminology varies, but normalization commonly refers to rescaling values to a bounded range such as 0 to 1, while standardization usually transforms a...",
      explanation: "Preprocessing choices often determine whether a model is reliable in production, so interviewers use these questions to test practical judgment.",
      sampleAnswer: "Terminology varies, but normalization commonly refers to rescaling values to a bounded range such as 0 to 1, while standardization usually transforms a feature to zero mean and unit variance using (x-mean)/standard deviation. Always state the exact transformation because 'normalization' can have different meanings in different contexts.",
      detailedAnswer: "Normalization and standardization are both feature-scaling techniques, but they transform data in different ways.\n\nNormalization commonly refers to min-max scaling, which maps values into a fixed range, often 0 to 1:\n\n```text\nx_normalized = (x - min(x)) / (max(x) - min(x))\n```\n\nStandardization transforms a feature using its mean and standard deviation:\n\n```text\nz = (x - mean(x)) / standard_deviation(x)\n```\n\nAfter standardization, the feature typically has mean approximately 0 and standard deviation approximately 1, although the values are not restricted to a fixed range.\n\nNormalization can be useful when a bounded range is desirable, for example in some neural-network inputs or algorithms where the input range matters. Standardization is often a better default for many statistical and machine-learning algorithms, especially linear models, SVMs, PCA, and gradient-based methods.\n\nExample:\nSuppose a feature contains values from 10 to 50. Min-max normalization maps 10 to 0 and 50 to 1. Standardization instead measures each value relative to the feature's mean and standard deviation.\n\nBoth techniques can be affected by outliers, but min-max scaling can be particularly sensitive because the minimum and maximum directly determine the transformation.\n\nThe most important production rule is the same for both: calculate scaling parameters from the training set only and reuse those parameters for validation, test, and production data.\n\nAlso, normalization is sometimes used loosely to mean other transformations, such as vector normalization. Therefore, in an interview, I would clarify that I am referring to min-max feature scaling when making this comparison.",
