      subcategorySlug: "regularization-and-optimization",
      experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
      difficulty: "MEDIUM",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "L1 regularization penalizes the sum of absolute coefficient values and can drive some coefficients exactly to zero, making it useful for sparse feature...",
      explanation: "This reveals whether the candidate understands why a model generalizes poorly and how to improve it rather than just naming an algorithm.",
      sampleAnswer: "L1 regularization penalizes the sum of absolute coefficient values and can drive some coefficients exactly to zero, making it useful for sparse feature selection. L2 penalizes squared coefficients and generally shrinks weights smoothly without forcing as many to zero. The appropriate choice depends on feature structure and model behavior.",
      detailedAnswer: "L1 and L2 regularization both penalize large model coefficients, but they use different penalty functions and therefore behave differently.\n\nL1 regularization adds the sum of absolute coefficient values:\n\n```text\nL1 penalty = ? × ?|w?|\n```\n\nL2 regularization adds the sum of squared coefficient values:\n\n```text\nL2 penalty = ? × ?w?²\n```\n\nThe major practical difference is that L1 can drive some coefficients exactly to zero. This makes L1 useful when feature selection or a sparse model is desirable. L2 generally shrinks coefficients toward zero without typically making many of them exactly zero.\n\nFor example, if a model has 1,000 features but only a smaller subset is useful, L1 may produce a sparse solution by effectively removing some features. L2 tends to distribute the influence across correlated features instead of eliminating as many completely.\n\nL1 is commonly associated with Lasso, while L2 is associated with Ridge regression. Elastic Net combines both penalties.\n\n```python\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import Lasso, Ridge, ElasticNet\n\nlasso = Lasso(alpha=0.1)       # L1\nridge = Ridge(alpha=0.1)       # L2\nelastic = ElasticNet(alpha=0.1, l1_ratio=0.5)\n```\n\nA practical consideration is correlated features. L1 may select one feature and drive another correlated feature to zero, sometimes making feature selection unstable. L2 is often more stable in such situations because it shrinks correlated coefficients rather than aggressively selecting one.\n\nInterview takeaway: L1 encourages sparsity and can perform feature selection; L2 smoothly shrinks coefficients and is often preferred when many features contribute useful information.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate understands the bias-variance trade-off and can explain how regularization controls overfitting.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
      followUpQuestions: ["What is regularization?", "What is the kernel trick in SVM?", "What is PCA and why use it?"],
