      shortDescription: "Batch gradient descent computes a gradient using the full training dataset for each update",
      explanation: "This tests whether the candidate understands core supervised learning concepts deeply enough to reason about model behavior.",
      sampleAnswer: "Batch gradient descent computes a gradient using the full training dataset for each update. Stochastic gradient descent updates from one example at a time, which is noisy but inexpensive per update. Mini-batch gradient descent uses small batches and is the common practical approach for neural networks because it balances computational efficiency and gradient stability.",
      detailedAnswer: "The three approaches differ mainly in how much training data is used to calculate each parameter update.\n\nBatch gradient descent uses the entire training dataset for every update. The gradient is therefore relatively stable and accurate, but each update can be expensive for large datasets.\n\nStochastic gradient descent, or SGD, uses one training example for each update. This makes individual updates very fast and introduces noise into the optimization process. That noise can sometimes help exploration, but the loss may fluctuate significantly.\n\nMini-batch gradient descent uses a small subset of the training data for each update, such as 32, 64, 128, or 256 examples. It provides a practical balance between computational efficiency and gradient stability and is the standard approach for training many neural networks.\n\nConceptually:\n```text\nBatch:       1 update -> entire dataset\nSGD:         1 update -> 1 example\nMini-batch:  1 update -> small batch\n```\n\nFor example, if there are 100,000 training examples and the batch size is 100:\n- Batch gradient descent processes all 100,000 examples before one update.\n- SGD performs 100,000 individual updates per epoch.\n- Mini-batch performs approximately 1,000 updates per epoch.\n\nMini-batch training also works efficiently with GPUs because matrix operations can process many examples in parallel.\n\nThe choice of batch size affects memory usage, throughput, gradient noise, and sometimes generalization. A larger batch can improve hardware utilization but requires more memory, while smaller batches generally produce noisier gradients.\n\nIn an interview, I would say mini-batch gradient descent is usually the practical default because it provides a good balance between stable optimization and computational efficiency.",
