      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "The learning rate controls how far optimization moves on each parameter update",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "The learning rate controls how far optimization moves on each parameter update. It is one of the most important training hyperparameters: too large can cause instability or divergence, while too small can make training extremely slow or get stuck before reaching a useful solution.",
      detailedAnswer: "The learning rate is a hyperparameter that controls how large each parameter update is during optimization. In gradient descent, it determines how far the model moves in the direction suggested by the gradient.\n\nThe basic update is:\n```text\n?_new = ?_old - ? × gradient\n```\n\nwhere `?` is the learning rate.\n\nIf the learning rate is very small, training can be stable but extremely slow. The optimizer may require many iterations to reach a good solution.\n\nIf the learning rate is too large, updates can overshoot the minimum. The loss may oscillate, fail to converge, or even become numerically unstable.\n\nThe ideal learning rate depends on the model, loss function, optimizer, feature scaling, batch size, and dataset. It is normally treated as a hyperparameter and selected using validation performance, learning-rate schedules, or techniques such as learning-rate warmup.\n\nFor example:\n```python\nfrom tensorflow.keras.optimizers import Adam\n\noptimizer = Adam(learning_rate=0.001)\n```\n\nModern optimizers such as Adam adapt the effective step size for individual parameters, but the overall learning-rate setting remains important.\n\nA useful production approach is to monitor training and validation loss rather than choosing a learning rate solely by convention. Learning-rate schedules can reduce the learning rate during training when progress slows.\n\nInterview takeaway: the learning rate controls the step size of optimization. Too small makes learning slow; too large can make training unstable.",
