      shortDescription: "Updates can overshoot useful minima, causing the loss to oscillate, diverge, or become NaN",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Updates can overshoot useful minima, causing the loss to oscillate, diverge, or become NaN. Training curves may show unstable loss rather than steady improvement. Reduce the learning rate or use a suitable learning-rate schedule/optimizer.",
      detailedAnswer: "If the learning rate is too high, the optimizer can take steps that are larger than the useful scale of the loss landscape. Instead of gradually approaching a minimum, it may overshoot it repeatedly.\n\nTypical symptoms include:\n- Training loss oscillates instead of decreasing smoothly.\n- Loss may increase rather than decrease.\n- The model fails to converge.\n- Validation performance may remain poor or become unstable.\n- Numerical values can become extremely large and eventually produce NaN or infinity.\n\nConsider a simple one-dimensional loss function. If the optimizer takes very small steps, it gradually moves toward the minimum. With a moderate learning rate, it reaches the minimum efficiently. With a very large learning rate, it can jump from one side of the minimum to the other and continue moving farther away.\n\nA simplified example is:\n```text\n?_new = ?_old - learning_rate × gradient\n```\n\nIf `learning_rate` is excessively large, even a reasonable gradient can produce an enormous parameter update.\n\nTo diagnose the problem, I would look at training-loss curves, gradient/parameter magnitudes, and whether reducing the learning rate makes optimization stable. I would also check input scaling and numerical issues because poor feature scales can make a seemingly reasonable learning rate behave badly.\n\nCommon fixes include:\n1. Reduce the learning rate.\n2. Use a learning-rate scheduler.\n3. Use an optimizer such as Adam when appropriate.\n4. Normalize or standardize features when the algorithm benefits from scaling.\n5. Use gradient clipping when exploding gradients are contributing to instability.\n6. Check for problematic data, extreme values, or numerical overflow.\n\nOne important distinction is that gradient clipping and lowering the learning rate solve related but different problems. Clipping limits unusually large gradients, while the learning rate controls the overall step size.\n\nInterview takeaway: a learning rate that is too high causes oversized updates, leading to oscillation, divergence, or numerical instability instead of convergence.",
