      sampleAnswer: "Logistic regression is a supervised classification model that applies a linear score to features and maps it through a logistic function to estimate a probability for binary classification. A threshold converts probability into a class. Despite its name, it is a classification model and is often a strong interpretable baseline.",
      detailedAnswer: "Logistic regression is a supervised learning algorithm primarily used for classification. Despite its name, it is a classification model rather than ordinary regression.\n\nFor binary classification, logistic regression first calculates a linear score:\n\n```text\nz = w?x? + w?x? + ... + b\n```\n\nIt then passes that score through the sigmoid function to produce a value between 0 and 1:\n\n```text\np = 1 / (1 + e^(-z))\n```\n\nThe output can be interpreted as the estimated probability of the positive class. A classification threshold, commonly 0.5 but not necessarily 0.5, is then used to convert the probability into a class prediction.\n\nFor example, a model predicting whether a transaction is fraudulent might output:\n\n```text\nP(fraud) = 0.87\n```\n\nIf the business threshold is 0.5, this would be classified as fraud. If missing fraud is much more costly than reviewing legitimate transactions, the threshold could be lowered to improve recall.\n\nLogistic regression is commonly trained by minimizing log loss, also called binary cross-entropy, rather than ordinary squared-error regression loss.\n\nAdvantages include:\n- Simple and fast.\n- Strong baseline for many classification problems.\n- Coefficients can provide useful directional information.\n- Produces probability estimates that can be calibrated when the modeling setup supports it.\n- Works well with high-dimensional sparse features such as text when combined with suitable preprocessing.\n\nLimitations include its basic linear decision boundary and sensitivity to feature representation. If the true relationship is highly nonlinear, a tree-based model or nonlinear model may perform better.\n\nRegularization such as L1 or L2 is commonly used to control overfitting.\n\nInterview takeaway: logistic regression models the probability of a class using a linear combination of features passed through a sigmoid for binary classification.",
