      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "A decision tree learns a sequence of feature-based splits and is easy to visualize but can have high variance",
      explanation: "Tree questions test both algorithm understanding and practical awareness of overfitting, feature importance, and model selection.",
      sampleAnswer: "A decision tree learns a sequence of feature-based splits and is easy to visualize but can have high variance. A random forest trains many randomized trees and aggregates their predictions, usually reducing variance and improving robustness. Forests are less interpretable than a single small tree and can be larger computationally.",
      detailedAnswer: "A decision tree is a single tree that recursively splits data based on feature conditions. A random forest is an ensemble of many decision trees whose predictions are combined.\n\nA decision tree is easy to understand and visualize. It can capture nonlinear relationships and feature interactions without requiring feature standardization. However, a fully grown tree can have high variance: small changes in the training data can produce a substantially different tree, and the tree may overfit.\n\nA random forest addresses this by training many trees using different bootstrap samples of the training data and randomized feature selection at splits. For classification, the trees typically vote; for regression, their predictions are averaged.\n\nComparison:\n\n```text\nDecision Tree\n- One model\n- Easy to interpret\n- Can capture nonlinear relationships\n- High risk of overfitting if uncontrolled\n- Lower computational cost\n\nRandom Forest\n- Many trees\n- Usually better generalization\n- Lower variance\n- More robust to individual tree errors\n- Less interpretable\n- More computationally expensive\n```\n\nRandom forests also generally require less careful hyperparameter tuning than some boosting methods and can provide useful feature-importance estimates, although importance measures should be interpreted carefully.\n\nFor example, for customer churn prediction, a single tree might make a very specific set of splits based on the training data. A random forest averages many different trees, making the final prediction less dependent on any one tree.\n\nBoth methods can handle nonlinear relationships and interactions, and neither generally requires standardization.\n\nInterview takeaway: a decision tree is one high-variance model, while a random forest reduces that variance by averaging many diversified trees.",
