      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Random forest primarily reduces variance through parallelized averaging of randomized trees",
      explanation: "Tree questions test both algorithm understanding and practical awareness of overfitting, feature importance, and model selection.",
      sampleAnswer: "Random forest primarily reduces variance through parallelized averaging of randomized trees. Gradient boosting builds trees sequentially to correct previous errors and often achieves stronger predictive accuracy on structured/tabular data with careful tuning. Random forests are frequently easier to tune robustly and can be less sensitive to certain settings; both should be compared using the same validation protocol.",
      detailedAnswer: "Random forest and gradient boosting are both tree-based ensemble methods, but they build their ensembles differently.\n\nRandom forest uses bagging. Many decision trees are trained independently using bootstrap samples and random subsets of features. Their predictions are then averaged or voted. Its main strength is variance reduction and robustness.\n\nGradient boosting is sequential. Each new tree is trained to improve the current ensemble by following the gradient of the loss. This allows the model to progressively correct previous errors, but it also makes the method more sensitive to hyperparameters and overfitting.\n\nComparison:\n\n```text\nRandom Forest\n- Trees trained independently\n- Bagging approach\n- Strong variance reduction\n- Usually robust and easier to tune\n- Can be parallelized naturally\n- Often a strong baseline\n\nGradient Boosting\n- Trees trained sequentially\n- Boosting approach\n- Each tree improves previous predictions\n- Often very strong on structured/tabular data\n- More sensitive to hyperparameters\n- Training is less parallel across boosting iterations\n```\n\nRandom forest generally works well when you want a robust model with relatively straightforward tuning. Gradient boosting can achieve higher predictive performance on many tabular problems when carefully tuned.\n\nGradient boosting usually has important interactions between learning rate, number of trees, tree depth, and regularization. Early stopping is often useful.\n\nNeither approach normally requires feature standardization because tree splits are based on feature thresholds rather than distance calculations.\n\nThe choice should ultimately be based on validation performance, latency, interpretability requirements, training cost, dataset characteristics, and operational constraints rather than assuming one algorithm is always better.\n\nInterview takeaway: random forest builds many independent randomized trees and averages them; gradient boosting builds trees sequentially to correct the current model's errors.",
