      sampleAnswer: "The kernel trick lets an SVM compute similarity in an implicit higher-dimensional feature space without explicitly constructing all transformed features. A kernel function calculates the required inner product directly. This allows nonlinear decision boundaries while avoiding potentially expensive explicit feature expansion.",
      detailedAnswer: "The kernel trick allows a Support Vector Machine to model nonlinear decision boundaries without explicitly transforming the original data into a potentially very high-dimensional feature space.\n\nA linear SVM finds a separating hyperplane in the input feature space. If the data is not linearly separable, we can conceptually map each input into a higher-dimensional feature space where a linear separator may exist.\n\nInstead of explicitly computing that transformation, the kernel trick computes the inner product between transformed points directly using a kernel function:\n\n```text\nK(x, z) = ?(x) - ?(z)\n```\n\nHere, `?` represents the implicit feature transformation.\n\nA common example is the RBF kernel:\n\n```text\nK(x, z) = exp(-? ||x - z||²)\n```\n\nThe SVM can therefore operate as if the data had been transformed into a higher-dimensional space without explicitly constructing all those features.\n\nCommon kernels include:\n- Linear kernel.\n- Polynomial kernel.\n- RBF/Gaussian kernel.\n- Sigmoid kernel.\n\nThe kernel trick is useful when the relationship between features and the target is nonlinear but the dataset size and computational requirements still make SVM practical.\n\nImportant SVM parameters include `C`, which controls the trade-off between margin violations and model complexity, and kernel-specific parameters such as `gamma` for the RBF kernel.\n\nA practical limitation is computational cost. Kernel SVMs can become expensive on very large datasets, so linear SVMs or other scalable models may be preferable.\n\nFeature scaling is also important for SVMs, particularly with distance-based kernels such as RBF.\n\nInterview takeaway: the kernel trick lets SVM compute similarities in an implicit high-dimensional feature space without explicitly creating that space.",
