      sampleAnswer: "Principal Component Analysis transforms correlated features into orthogonal principal components ordered by explained variance. It can reduce dimensionality, compress information, remove some redundancy, and help visualization. PCA components are linear combinations of the original features and may be less interpretable.",
      detailedAnswer: "PCA, or Principal Component Analysis, is an unsupervised dimensionality-reduction technique that transforms correlated features into a smaller set of new variables called principal components.\n\nThe first principal component captures the direction of maximum variance in the data. The second captures the maximum remaining variance subject to being orthogonal to the first, and subsequent components follow the same idea.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\nOriginal features\n      v\n   PCA transformation\n      v\nPrincipal components\n      v\nKeep the most useful components\n```\n\nPCA is commonly used for:\n- Reducing the number of features.\n- Compressing data while retaining much of its variance.\n- Removing redundancy from correlated features.\n- Making high-dimensional data easier to visualize.\n- Potentially reducing training time and memory usage.\n- Helping with numerical problems caused by highly correlated features in some models.\n\nFor example, if 100 original features contain substantial redundancy, PCA might represent much of the variation using 20 principal components.\n\nPCA is sensitive to feature scale, so standardization is often performed before PCA when features have different units or magnitudes.\n\nImportantly, PCA components are linear combinations of the original features, so they can be harder to interpret. PCA also does not use the target variable when finding components, meaning the directions with the highest variance are not necessarily the directions most useful for prediction.\n\nIn a machine-learning pipeline, PCA must be fitted only on training data. The learned transformation is then applied to validation, test, and production data.\n\nInterview takeaway: PCA reduces dimensionality by projecting data onto orthogonal directions that capture decreasing amounts of variance.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate can explain the core machine-learning concept accurately and relate it to a practical modeling decision.\\n\\n"],
