      shortDescription: "PCA is unsupervised and maximizes variance, not predictive signal",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "PCA is unsupervised and maximizes variance, not predictive signal. A low-variance feature can still be highly predictive, so discarding it may hurt accuracy. PCA can also reduce interpretability and may be inappropriate when the downstream model already handles correlated features effectively.",
      detailedAnswer: "PCA can hurt model performance when the dimensions with the highest variance are not the dimensions that contain the most predictive information.\n\nPCA is unsupervised: it looks at the structure and variance of the input features without considering the target variable. Therefore, a low-variance feature can be extremely important for prediction while a high-variance feature can contain mostly irrelevant variation.\n\nFor example, suppose a dataset contains:\n- Feature A: very large natural variation but almost no relationship with the target.\n- Feature B: small variation but a strong relationship with the target.\n\nIf PCA keeps only the first few high-variance components, information related to Feature B may be weakened or discarded.\n\nPCA can also hurt when:\n- The original features are already meaningful and interpretable.\n- The model benefits from individual feature identities.\n- The dataset is not sufficiently high-dimensional to justify dimensionality reduction.\n- The important relationship with the target is nonlinear and PCA's linear transformation does not capture it well.\n- The retained number of components is too small.\n- Scaling is inappropriate before PCA.\n- Sparse data is transformed into dense representations, increasing memory or computation costs.\n\nAnother important point is that PCA does not automatically remove noise. It removes directions with lower variance, but low variance does not necessarily mean noise.\n\nThe correct approach is to treat the number of components as a hyperparameter and evaluate it using a validation strategy. Rather than assuming 'retain 95% variance' is always optimal, compare predictive performance with and without PCA.\n\nInterview takeaway: PCA can hurt when variance does not align with predictive signal, because it optimizes reconstruction of input variance rather than prediction accuracy.",
