      sampleAnswer: "Dimensionality reduction maps high-dimensional data into fewer dimensions while attempting to preserve useful structure or information. It can reduce computation, noise, storage, or visualization complexity. Methods include PCA, feature selection, and nonlinear techniques such as UMAP or t-SNE for specific exploratory purposes.",
      detailedAnswer: "Dimensionality reduction is the process of reducing the number of input features or dimensions while attempting to preserve the information that is useful for analysis, visualization, or prediction.\n\nFor example, a dataset may contain 1,000 features, but many may be redundant, highly correlated, noisy, or unnecessary. Dimensionality reduction can transform those features into a smaller representation.\n\nThere are two broad approaches:\n\n1. Feature selection\nChoose a subset of the original features.\n\nExamples include:\n- Removing highly correlated features.\n- Statistical feature selection.\n- Recursive feature elimination.\n- Model-based feature selection.\n\n2. Feature extraction\nCreate new lower-dimensional features from the original ones.\n\nExamples include:\n- PCA.\n- Truncated SVD.\n- Autoencoders.\n- Learned embeddings.\n\nThe potential benefits include:\n- Lower memory usage.\n- Faster model training and inference.\n- Less redundant information.\n- Reduced risk of overfitting in some settings.\n- Easier visualization of high-dimensional data.\n- Potentially improved numerical stability.\n\nHowever, dimensionality reduction can also discard useful information and make models harder to interpret. Therefore, it should not be applied automatically.\n\nFor example, PCA might transform 100 original business features into 15 components. This reduces dimensionality, but the resulting components may no longer have an obvious business interpretation.\n\nIn a production ML pipeline, dimensionality-reduction parameters must be learned from training data only. The same transformation must then be applied consistently to validation, test, and production inputs.\n\nInterview takeaway: dimensionality reduction simplifies high-dimensional data by reducing the number of dimensions while trying to retain the information that matters for the task.",
