      subcategorySlug: "unsupervised-learning",
      experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
      difficulty: "MEDIUM",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "K-means partitions data into K clusters by iteratively assigning points to centroids and updating centroids, usually minimizing within-cluster squared...",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "K-means partitions data into K clusters by iteratively assigning points to centroids and updating centroids, usually minimizing within-cluster squared distance. Hierarchical clustering builds a tree of nested clusters and can be inspected as a dendrogram. K-means is efficient for suitable numeric data and roughly centroid-shaped clusters; hierarchical methods can reveal structure without committing to one K initially.",
      detailedAnswer: "K-means and hierarchical clustering are both unsupervised learning techniques used to group similar observations, but they approach clustering differently.\n\nK-means requires the number of clusters, K, to be specified in advance. It initializes K centroids, assigns each data point to its nearest centroid, recalculates the centroids, and repeats until the assignments or centroids stabilize.\n\nHierarchical clustering builds a hierarchy of clusters. In agglomerative hierarchical clustering, each observation initially forms its own cluster, and the closest clusters are progressively merged. The resulting hierarchy can be represented using a dendrogram, and the desired number of clusters can be selected afterward by cutting the dendrogram at an appropriate level.\n\nKey differences:\n\n```text\nK-means\n- Requires K beforehand\n- Uses centroids\n- Usually fast and scalable\n- Works well for compact, roughly spherical clusters\n- Sensitive to initialization and feature scaling\n\nHierarchical clustering\n- Does not require K initially\n- Produces a hierarchy/dendrogram\n- Can reveal nested cluster structure\n- More computationally expensive for large datasets\n- Choice of linkage affects the result\n```\n\nK-means commonly minimizes within-cluster squared Euclidean distance. Therefore, standardization is often important when features have different scales.\n\nHierarchical clustering can use different distance metrics and linkage methods such as single, complete, average, and Ward linkage. Ward linkage is closely related to minimizing within-cluster variance and generally assumes Euclidean geometry.\n\nFor a large dataset where I already have a reasonable idea of the number of compact clusters, I would usually consider K-means first. If understanding the hierarchy or nested structure is important and the dataset is manageable, hierarchical clustering can be more informative.\n\nInterview takeaway: K-means directly optimizes a chosen number of centroid-based clusters, while hierarchical clustering builds a tree of nested clusters that can be cut at different levels.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate can explain the core machine-learning concept accurately and relate it to a practical modeling decision.\\n\\n"],
