      subcategorySlug: "unsupervised-learning",
      experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
      difficulty: "MEDIUM",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "There is no universally correct K",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "There is no universally correct K. Common approaches include the elbow method, silhouette score, stability across resamples, domain requirements, and downstream usefulness. Evaluate whether clusters are meaningful and stable rather than choosing K solely because a metric has a small numerical advantage.",
      detailedAnswer: "Choosing K means deciding how many clusters provide a useful representation of the data. There is no universal value of K, so I would combine quantitative evaluation with domain knowledge.\n\nOne common approach is the elbow method. I run K-means for several values of K and calculate the within-cluster sum of squared errors, often called inertia. As K increases, the error decreases. I look for an 'elbow' where adding another cluster produces substantially smaller improvements.\n\nAnother approach is the silhouette score, which evaluates how well each observation fits within its assigned cluster compared with neighboring clusters. Higher values generally indicate better separation and cohesion.\n\nI would also consider:\n- Domain or business requirements.\n- Cluster interpretability.\n- Stability across different samples or initializations.\n- Whether clusters are useful for downstream decisions.\n- Whether the resulting cluster sizes are reasonable.\n\nExample:\n```python\nfrom sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n\nfor k in range(2, 11):\n    model = KMeans(n_clusters=k, random_state=42, n_init='auto')\n    model.fit(X)\n    print(k, model.inertia_)\n```\n\nI would not select K solely because it produces the highest mathematical score. For example, if K=7 produces slightly better separation but creates clusters that have no meaningful business interpretation, K=4 may be more useful.\n\nAlso, K-means assumes a particular geometry. If the data contains irregularly shaped clusters, changing K may not solve the underlying problem; another clustering algorithm may be more appropriate.\n\nInterview takeaway: I would use the elbow method and silhouette analysis as evidence, then validate the result against domain meaning, cluster stability, and downstream usefulness.",
