      subcategorySlug: "unsupervised-learning",
      experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
      difficulty: "MEDIUM",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "The silhouette score compares how close a point is to its own cluster versus the nearest alternative cluster",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "The silhouette score compares how close a point is to its own cluster versus the nearest alternative cluster. Scores range from -1 to 1; higher values generally indicate better-separated, more cohesive clusters. It is a diagnostic rather than proof that the clustering is meaningful.",
      detailedAnswer: "The silhouette score measures how well observations fit within their assigned clusters compared with the nearest alternative cluster. It considers both cluster cohesion and cluster separation.\n\nFor a data point, let:\n- `a` = average distance from the point to other points in its own cluster.\n- `b` = lowest average distance from the point to points in another cluster.\n\nThe silhouette coefficient is:\n\n```text\ns = (b - a) / max(a, b)\n```\n\nIts value is generally between -1 and 1.\n\nInterpretation:\n- Close to 1 -> the point is well matched to its own cluster and far from neighboring clusters.\n- Around 0 -> the point lies near a cluster boundary.\n- Below 0 -> the point may be assigned to the wrong cluster or the clustering structure may be weak.\n\nThe overall silhouette score is usually the average coefficient across observations.\n\nExample:\n```python\nfrom sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score\n\nscore = silhouette_score(X, labels)\nprint(score)\n```\n\nIt can be useful when comparing different K values in K-means or other clustering approaches.\n\nHowever, silhouette score has limitations. It depends on the distance metric and tends to favor certain cluster geometries, particularly compact and well-separated clusters. A low score does not necessarily mean the clustering is useless, especially when the underlying data has overlapping or non-convex structure.\n\nTherefore, I would combine silhouette score with visualization, domain knowledge, cluster stability, and downstream usefulness.\n\nInterview takeaway: silhouette score measures whether points are closer to their own cluster than to neighboring clusters, with higher values generally indicating better-defined clustering.",
