      sampleAnswer: "Naive Bayes applies Bayes' theorem while making a conditional-independence assumption among features given the class. The assumption is often unrealistic, but the resulting models can work very well and train quickly, especially for some text classification tasks. Variants include Gaussian, Multinomial, and Bernoulli Naive Bayes.",
      detailedAnswer: "Naive Bayes is a family of probabilistic classification algorithms based on Bayes' theorem combined with a simplifying assumption that features are conditionally independent given the class.\n\nBayes' theorem is:\n\n```text\nP(Class | Features) = P(Features | Class) × P(Class) / P(Features)\n```\n\nThe 'naive' assumption means that once the class is known, the model treats the features as conditionally independent. For features `x?, x?, ..., x?`, the likelihood is approximated as:\n\n```text\nP(x?, x?, ..., x? | Class)\n~= P(x? | Class) × P(x? | Class) × ... × P(x? | Class)\n```\n\nThis assumption is often unrealistic because real-world features can be correlated. Surprisingly, Naive Bayes can still perform very well, particularly when the classification decision does not require modeling all feature dependencies accurately.\n\nCommon variants include:\n- Gaussian Naive Bayes -> continuous features modeled using Gaussian distributions.\n- Multinomial Naive Bayes -> commonly used for count-based text features.\n- Bernoulli Naive Bayes -> useful for binary feature presence/absence.\n\nFor example, in spam classification, words such as 'free', 'offer', and 'winner' can provide evidence for the spam class. Naive Bayes estimates how likely those features are under spam versus non-spam and combines the evidence.\n\nA practical issue is zero probabilities. If a categorical feature value never appeared for a class during training, a probability can become zero and wipe out the entire product. Smoothing, such as Laplace smoothing, helps address this.\n\nAdvantages include fast training, low memory requirements, good performance on some high-dimensional sparse datasets, and probabilistic outputs.\n\nInterview takeaway: Naive Bayes applies Bayes' theorem while making the simplifying assumption that features are conditionally independent given the class.",
