      shortDescription: "Class imbalance occurs when one class has far more examples than another",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Class imbalance occurs when one class has far more examples than another. Accuracy can become misleading because a model can achieve high accuracy by mostly predicting the majority class. Evaluation and training strategies should reflect the cost of minority-class errors.",
      detailedAnswer: "Class imbalance occurs when one class has substantially more observations than another. For example, in fraud detection, 99.5% of transactions might be legitimate while only 0.5% are fraudulent.\n\nThe problem is that a model can achieve very high accuracy by mostly predicting the majority class while failing to identify the minority class.\n\nFor example, if only 1% of transactions are fraudulent, a model that predicts 'not fraud' for every transaction gets 99% accuracy but has 0% recall for fraud. That model is practically useless for fraud detection.\n\nClass imbalance can affect:\n- Training behavior.\n- Decision thresholds.\n- Probability estimates.\n- Evaluation metrics.\n- The model's ability to learn minority-class patterns.\n\nTherefore, accuracy alone is usually a poor metric for severely imbalanced classification.\n\nI would inspect metrics such as:\n- Precision.\n- Recall.\n- F1 or F-beta score.\n- PR-AUC.\n- Confusion matrix.\n- Class-specific costs.\n- Calibration when probabilities are used for decisions.\n\nThe correct response depends on the business problem. I might use class weighting, resampling, threshold optimization, or a combination of these approaches.\n\nIt is also important to use appropriate train/validation/test splits. For ordinary classification, stratification helps preserve class proportions. For temporal problems such as fraud, however, a time-aware split may be more appropriate than randomly mixing historical and future transactions.\n\nInterview takeaway: class imbalance means the class distribution is highly unequal, making naive accuracy misleading and requiring appropriate training and evaluation strategies.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate can explain the core machine-learning concept accurately and relate it to a practical modeling decision.\\n\\n"],
