      sampleAnswer: "Start with an evaluation metric aligned to the business cost, such as precision-recall measures, recall at a required precision, or expected cost. Techniques can include class weights, appropriate resampling, threshold tuning, anomaly-oriented approaches, and collecting better minority examples. Apply resampling only within training folds and validate on a representative distribution.",
      detailedAnswer: "For severe class imbalance, I would first understand the business cost of false positives and false negatives and establish an evaluation strategy before changing the training data.\n\nMy approach would typically include:\n\n1. Use appropriate metrics.\nI would avoid relying on accuracy and focus on minority-class precision, recall, F1/F-beta, PR-AUC, confusion matrix, and business costs.\n\n2. Use class weighting.\nMany algorithms allow higher weight to the minority class so that its mistakes contribute more to the training objective.\n\nExample:\n```python\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\nmodel = LogisticRegression(class_weight='balanced')\n```\n\n3. Consider resampling.\nOversampling the minority class or undersampling the majority class can change the effective training distribution. SMOTE and related methods can generate synthetic minority examples, but they must be applied only within the training data/folds.\n\n4. Tune the classification threshold.\nThe default 0.5 threshold is not necessarily optimal. A lower threshold can increase recall, while a higher threshold can improve precision.\n\n5. Use appropriate validation.\nFor ordinary classification, stratified splits are useful. For fraud or other time-dependent problems, I would preserve chronology to avoid training on future information.\n\n6. Check probability calibration.\nIf the model's probabilities drive monetary decisions, investigation priorities, or risk limits, I would evaluate whether predicted probabilities correspond to observed frequencies.\n\n7. Evaluate operational impact.\nFor example, if the system flags 10,000 transactions but investigators can review only 500, optimizing recall alone is not enough. Precision at the available review capacity may matter more.\n\nI would also investigate whether the minority class is being labeled consistently and whether there is leakage or sampling bias.\n\nA common mistake is applying SMOTE or oversampling before the train/test split. That can leak information between the training and evaluation sets and produce overly optimistic results.\n\nInterview takeaway: severe imbalance is not solved by blindly oversampling. I would combine appropriate metrics, cost-aware thresholding, suitable training strategies, leakage-safe validation, and business constraints.",
