      sampleAnswer: "Define business costs for false positives and false negatives first. Examine PR-AUC, precision, recall, precision at a chosen review capacity, recall at a required precision, calibration, and performance by important segments. Use a time-aware validation scheme where appropriate because fraud patterns and transaction distributions change over time.",
      detailedAnswer: "I would evaluate a fraud-detection model as a cost-sensitive classification system rather than optimizing accuracy alone. Fraud is usually rare, and false positives can create customer friction while false negatives represent financial loss.\n\nFirst, I would establish a realistic evaluation split. If fraud patterns change over time, I would use a time-based validation/test strategy so the model is evaluated on future-like transactions rather than randomly mixing future observations into training.\n\nThen I would evaluate:\n\n- Recall: how much actual fraud the system catches.\n- Precision: how many flagged transactions are actually fraudulent.\n- PR-AUC: useful for comparing ranking quality under severe class imbalance.\n- ROC-AUC: useful as a broad ranking metric, but often less informative than PR-AUC for rare fraud.\n- Confusion matrix: shows TP, FP, TN, and FN at a selected threshold.\n- Financial cost: expected fraud loss, investigation cost, customer-impact cost, and potentially prevented loss.\n- Calibration: whether predicted probabilities correspond reasonably to observed fraud rates when probabilities are used operationally.\n- Performance by segment: country, merchant type, transaction amount, device, customer segment, or other relevant populations.\n\nI would also examine the precision-recall curve and choose a threshold based on the actual investigation capacity and cost trade-offs.\n\nFor example, suppose the model identifies 90% of fraud but generates so many false positives that legitimate customers are constantly blocked. That may be worse operationally than a model with slightly lower recall but much better precision.\n\nI would also monitor the model after deployment because fraud patterns change. Important production metrics include fraud rate, false-positive rate, detection rate, chargeback or confirmed-fraud outcomes, latency, feature drift, and model performance over time.\n\nFinally, I would make sure the evaluation uses labels that become available after the appropriate fraud-confirmation delay. Otherwise, the evaluation can accidentally use information that would not have been available at prediction time.\n\nInterview takeaway: fraud models should be evaluated using PR-oriented metrics, cost-sensitive thresholding, realistic temporal validation, calibration, and post-deployment monitoring-not accuracy alone.",
