      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "The threshold should be selected using validation data and the actual cost/benefit of false positives and false negatives, not automatically set to 0.5",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "The threshold should be selected using validation data and the actual cost/benefit of false positives and false negatives, not automatically set to 0.5. Consider capacity constraints, desired precision/recall, calibration, and operational consequences. Freeze the threshold based on an appropriate validation period before final testing.",
      detailedAnswer: "I would choose a classification threshold based on the business cost of false positives and false negatives rather than automatically using 0.5.\n\nA classifier may output a probability such as:\n\n```text\nP(fraud) = 0.73\n```\n\nThe threshold determines whether that score becomes a positive prediction. Lowering the threshold generally catches more positives, increasing recall, but can also increase false positives. Raising it generally improves precision at the cost of missing more positives.\n\nI would typically:\n1. Generate predictions or probabilities on a validation set that was not used to fit the model.\n2. Evaluate precision, recall, F1/F-beta, cost, and operational metrics across candidate thresholds.\n3. Define the business objective, such as maximum recall subject to a precision constraint or minimum expected profit.\n4. Select the threshold based on that objective.\n5. Validate the chosen threshold on a separate test set.\n6. Monitor threshold performance after deployment.\n\nFor fraud detection, for example, I might optimize expected cost:\n\n```text\nExpected Cost = FN × Cost(FN) + FP × Cost(FP)\n```\n\nIf missing one fraudulent transaction costs much more than reviewing a legitimate transaction, the optimal threshold may be lower than 0.5.\n\nOperational constraints also matter. If investigators can review only 1,000 alerts per day, I may choose a threshold that keeps alert volume within that capacity or use a top-K/ranking strategy.\n\nThreshold selection should be separated from model training. I should not tune the threshold directly on the final test set because that turns the test set into a tuning dataset.\n\nAlso, a threshold only makes sense in the context of the model's score/probability behavior. If probabilities are poorly calibrated, threshold decisions based on probability interpretation can be misleading.\n\nInterview takeaway: choose the threshold on validation data according to business costs, desired precision/recall trade-offs, and operational constraints-not simply because 0.5 is the default.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate can explain the core machine-learning concept accurately and relate it to a practical modeling decision.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
      followUpQuestions: ["How do you choose K in K-means?", "How would you choose a baseline model?", "Why is linear regression not normally used for classification?"],
      tags: ["machine-learning", "choose", "classification", "threshold"],
      seoTitle: "How would you choose a classification threshold? | Machine Learning Interview Question",
      seoDescription: "The threshold should be selected using validation data and the actual cost/benefit of false positives and false negatives, not automatically set to 0.5",
      isPublished: true,
