      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Grid search evaluates every combination in a predefined grid, while random search samples combinations from specified distributions",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Grid search evaluates every combination in a predefined grid, while random search samples combinations from specified distributions. Random search can be more efficient when only a few hyperparameters strongly affect performance because it explores more unique values in important dimensions. Both require a sound validation protocol.",
      detailedAnswer: "Grid search and random search are hyperparameter-tuning techniques used to find a good combination of model settings, but they explore the search space differently.\n\nGrid search defines a fixed set of values for each hyperparameter and evaluates every possible combination. For example, if learning rate has 3 candidate values and tree depth has 4 values, grid search evaluates 3 × 4 = 12 combinations.\n\nRandom search samples combinations randomly from specified distributions or candidate ranges. Instead of evaluating every possible combination, it evaluates a fixed number of randomly selected configurations.\n\nComparison:\n\n```text\nGrid Search\n- Evaluates every specified combination\n- Simple and deterministic\n- Can become very expensive\n- Inefficient when many hyperparameters are irrelevant\n\nRandom Search\n- Samples configurations randomly\n- Usually explores large spaces more efficiently\n- Can discover good combinations without evaluating every possibility\n- Results depend on the random seed/sample count\n```\n\nRandom search is often more efficient because, in many problems, only a few hyperparameters have a major impact on performance. Grid search spends many evaluations varying less-important parameters while keeping important ones at suboptimal values.\n\nFor example, if a model has 10 hyperparameters but only learning rate and tree depth strongly affect performance, random search has a better chance of exploring a broad range of those important dimensions with the same computational budget.\n\nI would use grid search when the search space is small and well understood. I would usually prefer random search for a larger search space or when training is expensive.\n\nFor very expensive models, Bayesian optimization or successive-halving methods can be more efficient because they use previous evaluations to guide the search.\n\nThe validation procedure is more important than the search method itself. Hyperparameter tuning should use cross-validation or a dedicated validation set, while the final test set should remain untouched.\n\nInterview takeaway: grid search exhaustively evaluates a predefined grid, while random search samples configurations and often provides better coverage of large or high-dimensional search spaces for the same budget.",
