      subcategorySlug: "production-machine-learning",
      experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
      difficulty: "MEDIUM",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Early stopping halts training when validation performance stops improving according to a monitored metric or loss, usually with a patience window",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Early stopping halts training when validation performance stops improving according to a monitored metric or loss, usually with a patience window. It can reduce overfitting and unnecessary computation. The validation data used for stopping becomes part of model selection, so a separate test set should remain untouched for final evaluation.",
      detailedAnswer: "Early stopping is a regularization technique that stops model training when performance on a validation set stops improving. It is commonly used with iterative algorithms such as gradient boosting and neural networks.\n\nThe basic idea is that training performance can continue improving even after the model starts overfitting. Instead of allowing training to continue indefinitely, we monitor a validation metric and stop when improvement has stopped for a specified number of iterations.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nEpoch   Training Loss   Validation Loss\n1       0.60             0.62\n2       0.45             0.50\n3       0.35             0.42\n4       0.28             0.39\n5       0.23             0.40\n6       0.19             0.43\n```\n\nHere, validation loss reaches its best value around epoch 4 and then starts increasing. Continuing training would likely increase overfitting.\n\nA parameter called `patience` is commonly used. For example, with patience of 3, training can continue for three more iterations without improvement before stopping.\n\nIn gradient boosting, early stopping can determine how many trees should be added. In neural networks, it can determine how many epochs to train.\n\nExample:\n```python\nfrom tensorflow.keras.callbacks import EarlyStopping\n\ncallback = EarlyStopping(\n    monitor='val_loss',\n    patience=3,\n    restore_best_weights=True\n)\n```\n\nThe validation data must not be the final test set. The test set should remain untouched until the final evaluation.\n\nEarly stopping can reduce training time as well as overfitting, but stopping too aggressively can prevent the model from learning enough.\n\nInterview takeaway: early stopping monitors validation performance and stops iterative training when further training no longer improves generalization.",
