      experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
      difficulty: "MEDIUM",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Decision trees split using thresholds on individual features, so multiplying a feature by a positive constant does not fundamentally change the...",
      explanation: "Preprocessing choices often determine whether a model is reliable in production, so interviewers use these questions to test practical judgment.",
      sampleAnswer: "Decision trees split using thresholds on individual features, so multiplying a feature by a positive constant does not fundamentally change the ordering used to choose splits. Therefore tree-based models such as random forests and standard gradient-boosted trees generally do not require standardization. Scaling may still matter for preprocessing pipelines or models combined with distance-based methods.",
      detailedAnswer: "Tree-based models generally do not require feature scaling because their decisions are based on feature thresholds and ordering rather than distances or the magnitude of coefficients.\n\nFor example, a decision tree might learn:\n\n```text\nif age <= 30:\n    go left\nelse:\n    go right\n```\n\nSuppose age is transformed from years into months. The values change from 30 to 360, but the ordering of observations remains the same. The tree can simply choose a corresponding threshold, so the resulting split can represent the same partition of the data.\n\nThis applies to decision trees and generally to tree ensembles such as random forests and gradient-boosted trees.\n\nIn contrast, algorithms such as KNN, K-means, SVM with an RBF kernel, PCA, and many gradient-based models can be strongly affected by feature scale.\n\nFor example:\n```text\nIncome: 20,000 - 200,000\nAge:    18 - 80\n```\n\nA distance-based algorithm may allow income to dominate simply because its numerical scale is much larger. Tree splits do not have that same problem.\n\nThere are still reasons to preprocess tree-model inputs. Missing values, categorical variables, extreme data errors, inconsistent units, and model-specific implementation requirements may need attention. Some boosting libraries also have particular handling requirements for categorical features.\n\nAnother important point is that scaling can still be harmless in many tree-based workflows, but it usually does not provide the same benefit it provides for distance- or gradient-sensitive algorithms.\n\nInterview takeaway: trees care primarily about ordering and threshold-based partitions, so multiplying or shifting a feature usually does not change the split structure in the way it can change distance- or gradient-based optimization.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate understands how preprocessing affects model quality, data leakage, and the consistency of training and inference.\\n\\n"],
