      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Highly correlated features contain overlapping information and can make coefficient estimates unstable or harder to interpret in models such as linear...",
      explanation: "Preprocessing choices often determine whether a model is reliable in production, so interviewers use these questions to test practical judgment.",
      sampleAnswer: "Highly correlated features contain overlapping information and can make coefficient estimates unstable or harder to interpret in models such as linear regression. They can also dilute or duplicate feature importance in some tree ensembles. Correlation is not automatically harmful for predictive accuracy; its impact depends on the model and objective.",
      detailedAnswer: "Highly correlated features contain overlapping information. They are not automatically bad, but they can create problems for models whose parameters depend strongly on the relationship between predictors.\n\nThe biggest issue occurs with models such as linear regression and logistic regression. When two or more features are highly correlated, it becomes difficult for the model to determine how much of the prediction should be attributed to each feature independently.\n\nFor example, suppose a model uses:\n\n```text\nhouse_area_sqft\nhouse_area_sqm\n```\n\nThese features contain almost the same information. The model may assign unstable coefficients to them even though the overall predictions remain reasonably good.\n\nHighly correlated features can lead to:\n- Unstable coefficient estimates.\n- Large coefficient standard errors in statistical models.\n- Difficult interpretation.\n- Numerical instability in some optimization problems.\n- Redundant information and unnecessary model complexity.\n\nRegularization can reduce some of these effects. L2 regularization, for example, can stabilize coefficient magnitudes, while L1 may select among correlated features, although the selected feature can be unstable across samples.\n\nCorrelation is not equally problematic for every model. Tree-based models can often handle correlated features reasonably well for prediction, although feature-importance measures may become misleading because importance can be distributed among correlated features.\n\nPossible approaches include:\n- Remove redundant features.\n- Combine correlated features when domain meaning supports it.\n- Use regularization.\n- Apply dimensionality reduction such as PCA when appropriate.\n- Keep correlated features if they improve predictive performance and interpretability is not harmed.\n\nI would not automatically remove every correlated feature. The decision should depend on model type, prediction quality, interpretability requirements, and the business meaning of the features.\n\nInterview takeaway: correlation becomes a problem mainly when it makes parameter estimation or interpretation unstable; whether it matters depends heavily on the model and objective.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate understands how preprocessing affects model quality, data leakage, and the consistency of training and inference.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
