      sampleAnswer: "A neural network is a parameterized function composed of layers of units that apply weighted transformations and nonlinear activation functions. During training, backpropagation computes gradients and an optimizer updates weights to reduce a loss. Neural networks can learn complex representations but typically require careful data, optimization, regularization, and compute choices.",
      detailedAnswer: "A neural network is a machine-learning model made up of interconnected computational units called neurons, organized into layers. It learns a mapping from inputs to outputs by adjusting numerical parameters called weights and biases during training.\n\nA typical feed-forward neural network contains:\n- Input layer -> receives features.\n- Hidden layers -> transform the input through learned weights and nonlinear activation functions.\n- Output layer -> produces the final prediction.\n\nFor a neuron, the basic computation is:\n\n```text\nz = w?x? + w?x? + ... + b\noutput = activation(z)\n```\n\nThe activation function introduces nonlinearity. Common functions include ReLU, sigmoid, and tanh.\n\nDuring training, the model makes predictions, calculates a loss, computes gradients using backpropagation, and updates its weights using an optimizer such as SGD or Adam.\n\nFor example, in an image-classification model, early layers may learn simple patterns such as edges, later layers can combine them into shapes, and deeper layers can represent more complex visual patterns.\n\nNeural networks are powerful because multiple layers can learn hierarchical representations directly from data. They are widely used in computer vision, speech recognition, natural-language processing, recommendation systems, and many other applications.\n\nHowever, they can require substantial data and compute, and they can be sensitive to architecture, optimization, regularization, and preprocessing choices.\n\nInterview takeaway: a neural network learns complex functions by composing layers of weighted transformations and nonlinear activations, with the weights learned through optimization.",
