      experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
      difficulty: "MEDIUM",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Exploding gradients occur when backpropagated gradients become extremely large, causing unstable or divergent parameter updates",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Exploding gradients occur when backpropagated gradients become extremely large, causing unstable or divergent parameter updates. They can arise from repeated multiplication by factors greater than one, especially in deep or recurrent networks. Gradient clipping, better initialization, normalization, architecture choices, and an appropriate learning rate can help.",
      detailedAnswer: "Exploding gradients occur when gradients become extremely large during backpropagation. Instead of receiving useful-sized updates, model parameters can receive enormous updates, making training unstable or causing the loss to diverge.\n\nThe problem can occur when repeated derivatives or weight transformations amplify the gradient as it moves backward through many layers or time steps.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\ngradient ~= d? × d? × d? × ... × d?\n```\n\nIf these factors consistently amplify the signal, the gradient can grow exponentially.\n\nExploding gradients are particularly common in very deep networks and historically in recurrent neural networks processing long sequences.\n\nTypical symptoms include:\n- Training loss suddenly becomes extremely large.\n- Loss oscillates or diverges.\n- Parameter values become very large.\n- Gradients contain extremely large values.\n- Training eventually produces `NaN` or `Inf` values.\n\nCommon solutions include:\n\n1. Gradient clipping.\nLimit the gradient magnitude before applying the update.\n\n```python\nfrom torch.nn.utils import clip_grad_norm_\n\nclip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), max_norm=1.0)\n```\n\n2. Reduce the learning rate.\nA large learning rate can turn large gradients into excessively large parameter updates.\n\n3. Use appropriate initialization.\nPoor initialization can cause activations and gradients to grow or shrink through layers.\n\n4. Use suitable architectures and normalization techniques.\nResidual connections and normalization can improve optimization stability in appropriate architectures.\n\n5. Monitor numerical stability.\nCheck for extreme input values, unstable loss calculations, and overflow.\n\nGradient clipping is particularly useful when occasional gradients become unusually large, but it should not be used blindly to hide a fundamentally unstable model.\n\nInterview takeaway: exploding gradients occur when backpropagated gradients grow excessively large, causing huge parameter updates, unstable training, divergence, or numerical overflow.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate can explain the core machine-learning concept accurately and relate it to a practical modeling decision.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
      followUpQuestions: ["What causes vanishing gradients?", "How would you investigate a sudden production drop in model performance?", "What is model drift?"],
      tags: ["machine-learning", "causes", "exploding", "gradients"],
      seoTitle: "What causes exploding gradients? | Machine Learning Interview Question",
