      categorySlug: "machine-learning",
      subcategoryName: "Applied ML Scenarios",
      subcategorySlug: "applied-ml-scenarios",
      experienceLevel: "MID_LEVEL",
      difficulty: "MEDIUM",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Dropout randomly disables a fraction of units during training, encouraging the network not to rely too heavily on particular activations and acting as...",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Dropout randomly disables a fraction of units during training, encouraging the network not to rely too heavily on particular activations and acting as a form of regularization. During evaluation, dropout is disabled and the model uses the full network with appropriate scaling handled by the implementation.",
      detailedAnswer: "Dropout is a regularization technique used primarily in neural networks to reduce overfitting. During training, it randomly sets a fraction of activations to zero for each training step.\n\nFor example, with a dropout rate of 0.5, approximately half of the selected activations are randomly dropped during a training update.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\nNormal layer:\nA -> B -> C -> D -> E\n\nWith dropout:\nA -> B -> X -> D -> X\n```\n\nThe randomness prevents the network from relying too heavily on particular neurons or narrow combinations of features. It encourages the network to learn more distributed representations.\n\nA typical implementation is:\n```python\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\ndropout = nn.Dropout(p=0.5)\n```\n\nDuring training, dropout is active. During evaluation/inference, dropout is disabled and the framework applies the appropriate scaling behavior so that predictions remain consistent.\n\nDropout can be useful when a neural network has enough capacity to memorize the training data. However, excessive dropout can cause underfitting and slow convergence.\n\nThe appropriate dropout rate depends on the architecture and dataset. Modern architectures may use other regularization techniques, such as weight decay, data augmentation, early stopping, or architectural constraints, and dropout is not always necessary.\n\nA common implementation mistake is accidentally leaving dropout active during evaluation, which makes predictions unnecessarily stochastic.\n\nInterview takeaway: dropout reduces overfitting by randomly removing activations during training, forcing the network to learn representations that are less dependent on individual neurons.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate can explain the core machine-learning concept accurately and relate it to a practical modeling decision.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
      followUpQuestions: ["Data drift vs concept drift?", "How would you monitor a production ML model?", "How would you design an ML training pipeline?"],
      tags: ["machine-learning", "dropout"],
