      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Batch normalization normalizes intermediate activations using statistics computed from mini-batches during training and maintains running statistics...",
      explanation: "Preprocessing choices often determine whether a model is reliable in production, so interviewers use these questions to test practical judgment.",
      sampleAnswer: "Batch normalization normalizes intermediate activations using statistics computed from mini-batches during training and maintains running statistics for inference. It can stabilize optimization and sometimes permit higher learning rates. Its behavior depends on batch size and architecture, and alternatives such as LayerNorm are common in sequence/transformer models.",
      detailedAnswer: "Batch normalization is a technique that normalizes intermediate activations using statistics calculated from a mini-batch during training, followed by learned scale and shift parameters.\n\nFor a simplified feature, batch normalization first standardizes the activation using the batch mean and variance:\n\n```text\nx_normalized = (x - batch_mean) / sqrt(batch_variance + ?)\n```\n\nIt then applies learnable parameters:\n\n```text\noutput = ? × x_normalized + ?\n```\n\nwhere `?` and `?` allow the network to learn an appropriate scale and offset.\n\nBatch normalization can help make optimization more stable and often allows neural networks to train effectively with larger learning rates. It can also provide some regularization-like behavior because batch statistics introduce noise during training.\n\nA key detail is that training and inference behave differently. During training, batch statistics are used and running estimates are typically maintained. During inference, those stored running statistics are used rather than statistics from the current individual batch.\n\nExample:\n```python\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nlayer = nn.BatchNorm1d(128)\n```\n\nBatch normalization can be less convenient with very small batch sizes because batch statistics become noisy or poorly estimated. Its behavior also differs depending on the architecture and tensor dimensions.\n\nBatch normalization should not be confused with standardizing the original input features. Input standardization is preprocessing; batch normalization operates on intermediate network activations.\n\nInterview takeaway: batch normalization normalizes intermediate activations using batch statistics during training and learned parameters, helping stabilize and accelerate optimization in many neural-network architectures.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate understands how preprocessing affects model quality, data leakage, and the consistency of training and inference.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
