      difficulty: "MEDIUM",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "CNNs use local receptive fields and shared filters and have historically been effective for spatial data such as images",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "CNNs use local receptive fields and shared filters and have historically been effective for spatial data such as images. RNNs process sequences recurrently, maintaining a hidden state and historically serving tasks such as time-series and language modeling. Modern sequence systems often use attention/transformers instead of traditional RNNs for many tasks.",
      detailedAnswer: "CNNs and RNNs are neural-network architectures designed to capture different types of structure in data.\n\nCNNs, or Convolutional Neural Networks, use convolutional operations to detect local patterns. They are strongly associated with images and spatial data, although convolutions can also be used for sequences and other structured inputs.\n\nRNNs, or Recurrent Neural Networks, process sequences while maintaining a hidden state that carries information from earlier steps. They were traditionally used for text, speech, and time-series problems where sequence order matters.\n\nHigh-level comparison:\n\n```text\nCNN\n- Focuses on local patterns\n- Uses convolutional filters\n- Strong for spatial data\n- Computation can be highly parallelized\n- Can build hierarchical representations\n\nRNN\n- Processes sequentially\n- Maintains hidden state\n- Designed for ordered sequences\n- Historically useful for time-series/text\n- Sequential processing can limit parallelism\n```\n\nFor an image, a CNN can detect edges, textures, shapes, and increasingly complex visual structures through successive convolutional layers.\n\nFor a sequence, an RNN processes one step at a time and carries information through its hidden state:\n\n```text\nx? -> RNN -> h?\n           v\nx? -> RNN -> h?\n           v\nx? -> RNN -> h?\n```\n\nVanilla RNNs can suffer from vanishing and exploding gradients over long sequences. LSTM and GRU architectures were developed to improve the ability to retain useful information over longer sequences.\n\nTransformers have replaced RNNs in many modern NLP and sequence-learning applications because attention allows much more parallel processing and direct interaction between distant tokens.\n\nInterview takeaway: CNNs are primarily designed to exploit local spatial or local-pattern structure, while RNNs model ordered sequences using recurrent hidden states.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate can explain the core machine-learning concept accurately and relate it to a practical modeling decision.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
      followUpQuestions: ["How would you debug a model whose training accuracy is high but validation accuracy is poor?", "When would you prefer high recall over high precision?", "What happens if the learning rate is too high?"],
      tags: ["machine-learning", "high", "level"],
