      subcategoryName: "Applied ML Scenarios",
      subcategorySlug: "applied-ml-scenarios",
      experienceLevel: "EXPERIENCED",
      difficulty: "HARD",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "A feature pipeline includes ingestion, validation, transformation, feature computation, point-in-time correctness, handling of missing/invalid values,...",
      explanation: "Preprocessing choices often determine whether a model is reliable in production, so interviewers use these questions to test practical judgment.",
      sampleAnswer: "A feature pipeline includes ingestion, validation, transformation, feature computation, point-in-time correctness, handling of missing/invalid values, and delivery of features to training and serving systems. The same semantic definitions should be used across offline training and online inference where required. Feature freshness, lineage, and monitoring are important for production reliability.",
      detailedAnswer: "An ML feature pipeline is responsible for converting raw data into the features expected by the model in a consistent and reliable way for both training and production inference.\n\nIt can include:\n\n1. Data extraction\nRetrieve the required source data from databases, event streams, data warehouses, or feature stores.\n\n2. Data validation\nCheck schema, types, ranges, missingness, freshness, and invalid values.\n\n3. Cleaning\nHandle duplicates, missing values, invalid records, and other data-quality issues according to the modeling requirements.\n\n4. Transformations\nExamples include scaling, logarithmic transformations, tokenization, encoding, bucketing, and other deterministic transformations.\n\n5. Feature engineering\nCreate meaningful features such as aggregates, ratios, rolling statistics, time-based features, counts, or domain-specific signals.\n\n6. Feature selection or dimensionality reduction\nApply selected feature transformations when they are part of the trained pipeline.\n\n7. Learned preprocessing\nStore fitted imputers, scalers, encoders, PCA transformations, vocabularies, or similar artifacts generated from training data.\n\n8. Point-in-time correctness\nFor historical training data, every feature must represent information that would actually have been available at the prediction timestamp.\n\n9. Feature consistency\nTraining and serving should produce the same feature definitions, units, encodings, and semantics.\n\n10. Monitoring metadata\nTrack feature freshness, missingness, distributions, schema, and potentially lineage/version information.\n\nFor example, a customer-risk model might use:\n\n```text\nRaw transactions\n      v\nFilter valid events\n      v\nAggregate last 30 days\n      v\nCalculate transaction count\n      v\nCalculate average transaction amount\n      v\nEncode categorical attributes\n      v\nApply trained preprocessing\n      v\nModel-ready feature vector\n```\n\nA critical issue is leakage. If a feature uses information that became available after the prediction event, offline performance can look excellent while production performance fails.\n\nInterview takeaway: a feature pipeline should produce validated, point-in-time-correct, consistently transformed features and preserve the same semantics between training and serving.",
      keyPoints: ["Whether the candidate understands how preprocessing affects model quality, data leakage, and the consistency of training and inference.\\n\\n"],
      commonMistakes: [],
      followUpQuestions: ["What is feature engineering?", "What is feature importance?", "How would you design an ML training pipeline?"],
      tags: ["machine-learning", "belongs", "feature", "pipeline"],
      seoTitle: "What belongs in an ML feature pipeline? | Machine Learning Interview Question",
      seoDescription: "A feature pipeline includes ingestion, validation, transformation, feature computation, point-in-time correctness, handling of missing/invalid values,...",
      isPublished: true,
