      shortDescription: "A/B testing assigns comparable user traffic to different model versions and measures predefined business and model outcomes under real production...",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "A/B testing assigns comparable user traffic to different model versions and measures predefined business and model outcomes under real production conditions. Randomization, exposure consistency, guardrail metrics, sufficient sample size, and statistical analysis matter. Avoid testing on metrics that can be affected by the treatment itself without accounting for the resulting feedback loop.",
      detailedAnswer: "A/B testing for ML models compares two model versions by assigning users or requests to different variants and measuring their performance and business outcomes under real production conditions.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nTraffic\n  +-- 90% -> Model A\n  +-- 10% -> Model B\n```\n\nThe assignment should generally be controlled and consistent so that differences in outcomes can reasonably be attributed to the model rather than differences in traffic composition.\n\nBefore running the test, I would define:\n- Primary success metric.\n- Guardrail metrics.\n- Traffic allocation.\n- Experiment duration or sample requirement.\n- Eligibility criteria.\n- Randomization strategy.\n- Rollback criteria.\n\nFor a recommendation model, the primary metric might be conversion rate or revenue per user. Guardrails might include latency, error rate, customer complaints, or retention.\n\nFor a fraud model, the evaluation could involve detection rate, false-positive rate, prevented loss, investigation workload, and customer friction.\n\nImportant considerations include:\n\n1. Randomization.\nUsers should be assigned in a way that prevents systematic differences between groups.\n\n2. Stable assignment.\nA user should generally remain assigned to the same variant when appropriate so that their experience is not constantly switching.\n\n3. Statistical uncertainty.\nI would not declare a winner based on a tiny difference without considering sample size and uncertainty.\n\n4. Delayed labels.\nSome ML outcomes become known later. The experiment must account for that delay.\n\n5. Interference.\nIf one user's treatment affects another user's outcome, simple A/B assumptions may not hold.\n\n6. Safety guardrails.\nFor high-risk systems, I may start with shadow testing, canary deployment, or a very small traffic percentage before broad exposure.\n\nA/B testing differs from offline evaluation because the models are evaluated against real production traffic and real business outcomes.\n\nInterview takeaway: ML A/B testing is a controlled production experiment that compares model variants using predefined success and safety metrics while accounting for randomization, statistical uncertainty, and delayed outcomes.",
