      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "First define what constitutes a duplicate and determine whether duplicates occur within or across train/validation/test splits. Duplicates can overweight examples and, if the same entity appears across splits, create leakage and inflated validation scores. Deduplicate using a stable business key where appropriate, investigate why duplicates were created, and re-run evaluation after fixing the dataset.",
      detailedAnswer: "I would first determine whether the duplicate rows are genuine repeated observations, accidental duplicates, or legitimate repeated events. Removing duplicates blindly can destroy valid information.\n\nMy investigation would be:\n\n1. Define what makes a row unique.\nFor example, an event might be uniquely identified by `transaction_id`, while a customer table might use `customer_id`.\n\n2. Measure duplication.\nCheck exact duplicate rows and duplicates based on business keys.\n\n3. Determine when duplication was introduced.\nCompare raw data, transformed data, and final training data to identify the stage responsible.\n\n4. Check whether duplicates cross train and validation/test boundaries.\nThis is particularly important. If the same observation or near-identical event appears in both training and validation, validation performance can become artificially high.\n\n5. Investigate the effect on class distribution.\nIf duplicated rows disproportionately belong to one class, they can alter the effective class balance.\n\n6. Check whether duplicates change sample weights.\nRepeated observations effectively give those examples more influence during training.\n\n7. Re-run evaluation after fixing the issue.\nI would compare model performance before and after deduplication using the same appropriate evaluation strategy.\n\nFor example, if 10,000 transaction records accidentally contain the same transaction 20 times, the model may effectively see that transaction as much more important than other transactions.\n\nA particularly dangerous case is near-duplicate leakage. For example, multiple records from the same customer or almost identical events may be split across training and validation. The model may appear to generalize well because validation examples are extremely similar to training examples.\n\nI would also check whether duplicates are legitimate repeated events. In transactional systems, two identical-looking purchases can be separate real transactions and should not necessarily be removed.\n\nThe fix should usually happen upstream in the data pipeline rather than only deleting duplicates inside the training notebook.\n\nInterview takeaway: duplicate-row investigation starts by defining the correct business key, determining whether repetition is legitimate, checking train/validation contamination, and measuring the impact on model behavior before deciding whether to deduplicate.",
