      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "Choose a simple, fast, transparent method that is appropriate for the task and establishes a credible performance floor",
      explanation: "Fundamental questions expose gaps in first principles and show how clearly the candidate reasons about machine learning.",
      sampleAnswer: "Choose a simple, fast, transparent method that is appropriate for the task and establishes a credible performance floor. The baseline should use the same data split and evaluation metric as later models. A baseline helps determine whether added complexity actually provides meaningful value.",
      detailedAnswer: "A baseline model should be simple, fast, reproducible, and strong enough to provide a meaningful reference point for more sophisticated approaches.\n\nI would start by understanding the prediction task and selecting an appropriate baseline.\n\nExamples:\n- Classification -> majority-class predictor, logistic regression, or a simple tree.\n- Regression -> mean/median predictor or linear regression.\n- Text classification -> simple bag-of-words plus logistic regression.\n- Time-series forecasting -> naive previous-value or seasonal-naive forecast.\n\nI would establish a baseline before investing heavily in complex models.\n\nFor example, in binary classification with an imbalanced dataset, a majority-class predictor might achieve high accuracy but poor recall. Therefore, I would report appropriate metrics rather than relying on accuracy alone.\n\nA useful baseline should measure:\n\n```text\nPredictive metric\nTraining time\nInference latency\nMemory/resource usage\nOperational complexity\n```\n\nThen I can ask whether the more sophisticated model provides a meaningful improvement.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nLogistic regression -> PR-AUC 0.80\nGradient boosting   -> PR-AUC 0.87\n```\n\nThe second model has a measurable improvement. I can then evaluate whether that improvement justifies its additional complexity.\n\nI would also make sure the baseline uses the same realistic data split and avoids leakage. A weak or incorrectly evaluated baseline can make a complex model appear better than it actually is.\n\nFor production systems, the current production model can also serve as an important baseline. A new candidate should generally demonstrate a meaningful improvement or another compelling operational advantage before replacing it.\n\nInterview takeaway: choose a simple, transparent baseline that reflects the task, establishes realistic performance, and provides a reference against which model complexity and improvement can be justified.",
