      sampleAnswer: "Measure and understand label noise before changing the model. Review labeling rules, sample disagreements, identify systematic noise, and consider relabeling high-value examples. Robust losses, soft labels, filtering, or noise-aware methods can help depending on the source. Evaluate against a cleaner gold set when possible.",
      detailedAnswer: "Noisy labels are incorrect, inconsistent, ambiguous, or unreliable target values in the training data. They can limit model performance because the model is being trained to reproduce incorrect supervision.\n\nI would first determine the source and type of label noise rather than immediately changing the model.\n\nMy approach would be:\n\n1. Audit the labeling process.\nUnderstand how labels are generated, who or what creates them, and whether different labeling rules are being applied.\n\n2. Measure label quality.\nManually review a representative sample, especially examples where the model is highly uncertain or consistently disagrees with labels.\n\n3. Identify systematic noise.\nFor example, one data source or customer segment may have significantly worse labeling quality.\n\n4. Improve the labeling process.\nClarify labeling guidelines, add validation rules, use multiple annotators when appropriate, and resolve disagreements.\n\n5. Use robust training techniques.\nDepending on the problem, approaches can include label smoothing, robust loss functions, sample weighting, noise-aware methods, or methods that identify suspicious examples.\n\n6. Avoid overfitting to noisy examples.\nRegularization, early stopping, data augmentation, and appropriate model capacity can help.\n\n7. Use clean evaluation data.\nIf the test set contains substantial label noise, the measured model performance itself becomes unreliable. I would invest in creating a high-quality evaluation set where possible.\n\n8. Investigate ambiguous examples.\nSome apparent label noise is actually ambiguity in the task definition. If two experts cannot consistently agree on a label, the problem may need a clearer target definition.\n\nFor example, if a fraud label is assigned based on a chargeback process, the label may not be available immediately and may contain legitimate edge cases. I would understand the label-generation process before treating disagreements as model errors.\n\nI would not simply delete every example where the model disagrees with the label. A model can be wrong, and removing difficult examples can introduce selection bias.\n\nInterview takeaway: noisy labels are primarily a data-quality problem. I would identify the source, quantify it, improve the labeling process and evaluation set, and then use appropriate robust modeling techniques where necessary.",
