      experienceLevel: "EXPERIENCED",
      difficulty: "HARD",
      interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
      shortDescription: "First determine whether the business actually requires higher recall and what false-positive capacity is acceptable",
      explanation: "This checks practical model assessment skills and whether the candidate can avoid relying on a misleading metric.",
      sampleAnswer: "First determine whether the business actually requires higher recall and what false-positive capacity is acceptable. If so, lower the decision threshold, tune class weighting or the training objective, improve minority-class features/data, and evaluate using precision-recall tradeoffs. Do not change the threshold on the test set repeatedly; use validation data.",
      detailedAnswer: "A model with high precision but poor recall is conservative: when it predicts the positive class, it is usually correct, but it misses many actual positive cases.\n\nI would first determine whether low recall is actually a problem for the business. If false negatives are expensive, I would try to increase recall while controlling the resulting increase in false positives.\n\nMy approach would be:\n\n1. Inspect the confusion matrix.\nDetermine how many positive examples are being missed and whether the errors are concentrated in specific segments.\n\n2. Check the classification threshold.\nIf the threshold is too high, lowering it will generally increase recall and reduce precision.\n\n3. Evaluate the precision-recall curve.\nChoose an operating point that reflects the business cost of false positives and false negatives.\n\n4. Consider class weighting.\nTrain the model with higher weight for the minority/positive class when appropriate.\n\n5. Investigate the training data.\nCheck whether positive examples are underrepresented, poorly labeled, or missing important patterns.\n\n6. Improve features.\nPoor recall can indicate that the model lacks useful signals needed to identify difficult positive examples.\n\n7. Tune the model.\nTry appropriate model complexity, regularization, or alternative algorithms while using leakage-safe validation.\n\n8. Consider a two-stage system.\nFor example:\n\n```text\nBroad high-recall detector\n        v\nMore precise secondary classifier\n        v\nHuman review / final action\n```\n\nThis can be useful when missing a positive case is costly but the downstream action is expensive.\n\nI would avoid optimizing recall in isolation. If lowering the threshold causes thousands of false alerts, the resulting system may be operationally worse.\n\nFor example, in fraud detection, increasing recall from 70% to 95% sounds excellent, but if precision falls so far that investigators cannot review the resulting alerts, the change may not be useful.\n\nInterview takeaway: with poor recall and good precision, I would first examine threshold and error costs, then consider class weighting, better features, model tuning, and potentially a staged detection pipeline.",
