      detailedAnswer: "I would treat this as a potential train-serving mismatch, distribution-shift, leakage, or evaluation-design problem rather than assuming the production model suddenly became bad.\n\nMy investigation would follow the complete path from training data to production predictions.\n\n1. Verify the production metric.\nMake sure production labels are correct, sufficiently mature, and calculated consistently with the offline metric.\n\n2. Check for data leakage in offline evaluation.\nA feature may have contained information that would not actually be available at prediction time.\n\n3. Check train-serving skew.\nCompare training and production preprocessing, encoders, feature definitions, units, defaults, missing-value handling, and transformations.\n\n4. Check feature availability and freshness.\nA production feature may be missing, delayed, stale, or generated differently from the training version.\n\n5. Compare distributions.\nLook for data drift, changes in class prevalence, new categories, and changes in important features.\n\n6. Check temporal effects.\nIf the model was evaluated using random splitting but production represents future data, the offline evaluation may have been unrealistically optimistic.\n\n7. Check population differences.\nProduction may contain users, devices, geographies, or traffic sources that were poorly represented in training.\n\n8. Verify model and preprocessing versions.\nMake sure production is actually running the model that was evaluated offline.\n\n9. Check threshold and decision logic.\nThe underlying model may still rank examples well, while production applies a different threshold or business rule.\n\n10. Check infrastructure behavior.\nTimeouts, feature-service failures, fallbacks, stale caches, and default feature values can all change predictions.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nOffline:\nModel receives complete feature vector\n        v\nPR-AUC = excellent\n\nProduction:\nFeature service times out\n        v\nSeveral features become defaults/null\n        v\nPrediction quality drops\n```\n\nOnce the root cause is identified, I would mitigate the issue through rollback, feature-pipeline correction, retraining on representative data, threshold adjustment, or another targeted fix.\n\nI would then add a regression test or monitoring check so that the same failure is detected earlier in the future.\n\nInterview takeaway: excellent offline performance with poor production results usually points to leakage, distribution shift, train-serving skew, feature availability problems, incorrect evaluation design, or deployment/configuration differences.",
